1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,086
In western India there is a temple that was never built.

2
00:00:04,086 --> 00:00:05,895
Nobody laid a single stone.

3
00:00:05,895 --> 00:00:13,199
It was cut downward out of one living hill, until a whole Shiva temple stood free in a pit of its own making.

4
00:00:13,666 --> 00:00:14,275
Kailasa.

5
00:00:14,275 --> 00:00:16,026
Cave sixteen at Ellora.

6
00:00:16,026 --> 00:00:27,366
Its tower rises more than thirty metres above the courtyard, and the courtyard itself is about eighty metres long, carved out of solid volcanic rock.

7
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:30,773
Online, you will hear a different story.

8
00:00:30,773 --> 00:00:35,329
Four hundred thousand tons of rock, removed in eighteen years.

9
00:00:35,329 --> 00:00:36,652
Five tons an hour.

10
00:00:36,652 --> 00:00:39,004
Impossible even today, they say.

11
00:00:39,004 --> 00:00:43,266
So it must have been lasers, energy machines, or visitors.

12
00:00:43,733 --> 00:00:51,010
So let's rewind this hill to bare rock, set up an imaginary time-lapse camera, and run the clock forward.

13
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:56,000
At every stage of the build, we test the alien claim that belongs there.

14
00:00:56,466 --> 00:00:57,411
Eight stages.

15
00:00:57,411 --> 00:00:58,065
One hill.

16
00:00:58,065 --> 00:01:01,118
And at the end, a scorecard and a verdict.

17
00:01:01,118 --> 00:01:04,607
Respectfully, because this is not only a puzzle.

18
00:01:04,607 --> 00:01:06,133
It is a sacred place.

19
00:01:10,599 --> 00:01:12,059
Stage one: the hill.

20
00:01:12,059 --> 00:01:14,540
Before any chisel, there was lava.

21
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:21,839
Around sixty-five million years ago, eruptions spread layer after layer of basalt across the Deccan.

22
00:01:21,839 --> 00:01:27,166
Geologists call it the Deccan Traps, from a Scandinavian word for stairs.

23
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:36,126
The Archaeological Survey of India notes that this basalt is relatively workable when freshly cut and hardens as it weathers.

24
00:01:36,126 --> 00:01:41,833
And the builders chose fine-grained layers, following natural cracks to save labour.

25
00:01:42,299 --> 00:01:44,734
This hill was not new to carvers.

26
00:01:44,734 --> 00:01:55,433
Ellora holds thirty-four famous caves along two kilometres of cliff: Buddhist, Hindu and Jain, cut between roughly the sixth and tenth centuries.

27
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:59,180
And Ellora came late in a longer story.

28
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:10,366
India's rock-cut tradition runs from the Barabar caves of the third century BCE through the great halls of Karla, Kanheri and Ajanta.

29
00:02:10,833 --> 00:02:15,177
Maharashtra alone has roughly twelve hundred rock-cut caves.

30
00:02:15,177 --> 00:02:20,969
By the eighth century, carving architecture out of cliffs was not a lost secret.

31
00:02:20,969 --> 00:02:24,300
It was a profession, eight hundred years deep.

32
00:02:24,766 --> 00:02:27,789
The alien argument usually skips this part.

33
00:02:27,789 --> 00:02:31,375
It treats Kailasa as if it appeared out of nowhere.

34
00:02:31,375 --> 00:02:36,366
On our time-lapse, it appears at the very end of a long apprenticeship.

35
00:02:36,833 --> 00:02:42,880
So at year zero we have the right rock and generations of people who knew how to cut it.

36
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:45,766
What we need next is a patron, and a plan.

37
00:02:46,233 --> 00:02:48,010
Stage two: the blueprint.

38
00:02:48,010 --> 00:02:53,695
In the mid-eighth century, a new dynasty took over the Deccan: the Rashtrakutas.

39
00:02:53,695 --> 00:02:58,599
Their founder, Dantidurga, left an inscription in the cave next door.

40
00:02:59,066 --> 00:03:02,596
The temple itself carries no founding inscription.

41
00:03:02,596 --> 00:03:15,233
But a royal copper-plate grant, written around eight hundred and twelve, credits a king called Krishnaraja with a marvellous temple on the hill at Elapura, the old name of Ellora.

42
00:03:15,699 --> 00:03:17,884
And it says something remarkable.

43
00:03:17,884 --> 00:03:22,320
The celestial beings were amazed, as if the temple had made itself.

44
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:26,491
And even the architect who made it wondered how he had done it.

45
00:03:26,491 --> 00:03:30,066
The builders' own descendants were already astonished.

46
00:03:30,533 --> 00:03:38,597
Most scholars read Krishnaraja as Krishna the First, who reigned from about seven fifty-six to seven seventy-three.

47
00:03:38,597 --> 00:03:40,911
The link isn't perfectly certain.

48
00:03:40,911 --> 00:03:44,066
But no other builder has any evidence at all.

49
00:03:44,533 --> 00:03:47,049
Here is the clue to the blueprint.

50
00:03:47,049 --> 00:04:01,333
The main shrine closely resembles, on a bigger scale, the Virupaksha temple at Pattadakal, built by the Chalukyas a few years earlier, itself modelled on the Kailasanatha temple at Kanchipuram.

51
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:02,972
Archaeologist M.

52
00:04:02,972 --> 00:04:03,119
K.

53
00:04:03,119 --> 00:04:10,303
Dhavalikar argued that after defeating the Chalukyas, Krishna brought their temple builders north.

54
00:04:10,303 --> 00:04:14,701
If so, the team arrived with a proven design in their heads.

55
00:04:14,701 --> 00:04:16,460
They were not inventing.

56
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:17,999
They were scaling up.

57
00:04:18,466 --> 00:04:23,411
Dhavalikar also noticed that no major part looks like an afterthought.

58
00:04:23,411 --> 00:04:26,732
The whole complex seems planned from the start.

59
00:04:26,732 --> 00:04:29,134
Where are the plans, skeptics ask?

60
00:04:29,134 --> 00:04:33,513
Eighth-century working drawings almost never survive anywhere.

61
00:04:33,513 --> 00:04:35,633
The temple itself is the plan.

62
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:49,656
A legend, written down centuries later, says a queen vowed to fast until she saw the top of a new temple, and an architect named Kokasa promised it within a week, by carving from the top of a rock.

63
00:04:49,656 --> 00:04:54,266
Later inscriptions do mention a family of architects called Kokasa.

64
00:04:54,733 --> 00:04:56,573
Stage three: the trenches.

65
00:04:56,573 --> 00:04:58,342
The camera starts to run.

66
00:04:58,342 --> 00:05:02,801
Workers level the top and mark the temple's outline from above.

67
00:05:02,801 --> 00:05:08,533
Then they cut down: three deep trenches forming a giant U around a block of rock.

68
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,829
The block in the middle becomes the temple.

69
00:05:11,829 --> 00:05:19,066
The trenches become the courtyard, about eighty-two by forty-six metres, up to thirty metres deep at the back.

70
00:05:19,533 --> 00:05:23,172
Now, the first alien claim: lasers and energy beams.

71
00:05:23,172 --> 00:05:26,251
If that were true, the walls should show it.

72
00:05:26,251 --> 00:05:32,199
Instead, they are covered in chisel and pick marks, on surfaces all over the complex.

73
00:05:32,666 --> 00:05:36,977
Rows of overlapping strokes, each one a blow by a human arm.

74
00:05:36,977 --> 00:05:40,138
A laser would leave melted, glassy surfaces.

75
00:05:40,138 --> 00:05:41,215
There are none.

76
00:05:41,215 --> 00:05:44,233
The builders signed their work everywhere.

77
00:05:44,699 --> 00:05:47,763
And the basalt here is layered and jointed.

78
00:05:47,763 --> 00:05:54,033
Split along those natural cracks with wedges and levers, large blocks come away at once.

79
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:57,666
You don't have to grind every gram of rock to dust.

80
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:03,468
Working from the top down has another advantage that sounds backwards at first.

81
00:06:03,468 --> 00:06:05,292
You never need scaffolding.

82
00:06:05,292 --> 00:06:09,141
Each worker stands on the rock that has not been cut yet.

83
00:06:09,141 --> 00:06:11,100
The hill is its own platform.

84
00:06:11,566 --> 00:06:17,584
Some websites go further: acid that melted the rock, sound waves that shattered it.

85
00:06:17,584 --> 00:06:21,500
None comes with a sample, a residue, or a single test.

86
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:28,315
At the end of stage three, our time-lapse shows a raw block of stone standing in a trench.

87
00:06:28,315 --> 00:06:29,303
No temple yet.

88
00:06:29,303 --> 00:06:31,066
Just a block, and a plan.

89
00:06:31,533 --> 00:06:33,015
Stage four: the spire.

90
00:06:33,015 --> 00:06:36,047
And here is the strangest part of the method.

91
00:06:36,047 --> 00:06:40,966
The carvers began with the top of the temple, the tower, and worked down.

92
00:06:41,433 --> 00:06:47,120
So the crown of the temple was finished while the ground floor was still solid rock.

93
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:52,333
Each level was roughed out, sculpted, and left behind as the work moved down.

94
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,487
It also means there is no undo button.

95
00:06:55,487 --> 00:06:59,024
You can't glue a broken nose back onto a monolith.

96
00:06:59,024 --> 00:07:04,399
Every cut has to be right, which is why planning matters more than any tool.

97
00:07:04,866 --> 00:07:09,352
And we don't have to imagine a half-finished top-down temple.

98
00:07:09,352 --> 00:07:18,766
Far to the south, at Kalugumalai, stands Vettuvan Koil, an eighth-century Shiva temple carved from the top of a granite hillock.

99
00:07:19,233 --> 00:07:20,749
It was never finished.

100
00:07:20,749 --> 00:07:26,470
Its tower and upper sculptures are complete, but the lower part was never cut free.

101
00:07:26,470 --> 00:07:28,399
A time-lapse frozen halfway.

102
00:07:28,866 --> 00:07:34,402
Historians even compare it with Pattadakal, Kanchipuram and Ellora.

103
00:07:34,402 --> 00:07:37,871
Same design family, same era, same method.

104
00:07:37,871 --> 00:07:42,333
Human workshops sharing a style across southern India.

105
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:46,041
So the top-down method needs no outside help.

106
00:07:46,041 --> 00:07:50,362
It is a known technique with a surviving unfinished example.

107
00:07:50,362 --> 00:07:53,747
What impresses is the scale and the discipline.

108
00:07:53,747 --> 00:07:54,899
Not the physics.

109
00:07:55,366 --> 00:07:56,879
Stage five: the rubble.

110
00:07:56,879 --> 00:08:01,548
This is the heart of the alien case, so let's do the numbers carefully.

111
00:08:01,548 --> 00:08:05,100
How much rock came out, how fast, and where did it go?

112
00:08:05,566 --> 00:08:08,994
The popular claim is four hundred thousand tons.

113
00:08:08,994 --> 00:08:15,491
The commonly cited estimate is three million cubic feet, about two hundred thousand tonnes.

114
00:08:15,491 --> 00:08:19,633
Britannica says one hundred fifty to two hundred thousand.

115
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:26,616
The podcast Skeptoid points out that the TV show Ancient Aliens simply doubled the number.

116
00:08:26,616 --> 00:08:28,789
And the famous eighteen years?

117
00:08:28,789 --> 00:08:33,133
It isn't in any inscription, chronicle or excavation report.

118
00:08:33,133 --> 00:08:37,333
It is a modern guess, repeated until it sounded like fact.

119
00:08:37,799 --> 00:08:40,493
But let's accept the worst case anyway.

120
00:08:40,493 --> 00:08:45,396
Four hundred thousand tons in eighteen years is about sixty tons a day.

121
00:08:45,396 --> 00:08:50,852
Across five hundred workers, that's around a hundred and twenty kilograms each.

122
00:08:50,852 --> 00:08:53,200
A few baskets per person, per day.

123
00:08:53,666 --> 00:08:57,345
And most of that rock didn't have to be lifted at all.

124
00:08:57,345 --> 00:09:04,089
One academic reconstruction describes large boulders split from the hill and rolled down the slope.

125
00:09:04,089 --> 00:09:06,133
Gravity did the heavy lifting.

126
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:12,267
Dhavalikar estimated that one worker can cut about four cubic feet of rock a day.

127
00:09:12,267 --> 00:09:20,733
With two hundred and fifty labourers, the main excavation takes years, not centuries: he suggested about five and a half.

128
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:22,872
So where did it all go?

129
00:09:22,872 --> 00:09:24,762
Here we have to be honest.

130
00:09:24,762 --> 00:09:29,197
No published excavation has traced the spoil heap of Kailasa.

131
00:09:29,197 --> 00:09:31,233
That part is genuinely open.

132
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:34,097
But open doesn't mean impossible.

133
00:09:34,097 --> 00:09:39,400
The temple faces a valley, with the Elaganga stream and the plains below.

134
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:45,866
Rock rolled into a river valley twelve centuries ago does not stay in a neat pile for us.

135
00:09:46,333 --> 00:09:53,007
Think of monsoon floods, farming, roads, and twelve hundred years of people needing good stone.

136
00:09:53,007 --> 00:09:56,028
And this hill holds around a hundred caves.

137
00:09:56,028 --> 00:09:58,276
Their rubble went somewhere too.

138
00:09:58,276 --> 00:10:00,033
Nobody calls those alien.

139
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:03,829
The missing rubble is a real gap in the record.

140
00:10:03,829 --> 00:10:05,670
But a gap is not evidence.

141
00:10:05,670 --> 00:10:09,000
It is simply a question nobody has dug for yet.

142
00:10:09,466 --> 00:10:10,911
Stage six: the halls.

143
00:10:10,911 --> 00:10:23,433
With the outside shaped, the carvers hollowed the temple from within: a great hall on sixteen pillars, a sanctum, porches, and inside the courtyard walls, three storeys of galleries.

144
00:10:23,899 --> 00:10:29,115
Around the base, life-size elephants seem to carry the temple on their backs.

145
00:10:29,115 --> 00:10:36,633
Stone bridges, left in the living rock, link the gateway, the pavilion of the sacred bull Nandi, and the porch.

146
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:47,899
Which brings us to the second alien claim, from television: that these caves lead to an underground super-cave holding energy machines from a world before ours.

147
00:10:48,366 --> 00:10:50,967
No such chamber has ever been found.

148
00:10:50,967 --> 00:10:53,351
And Ellora was never a lost site.

149
00:10:53,351 --> 00:11:04,766
A tenth-century Arab geographer described it, a sultan repaired the road here in thirteen fifty-two, and the Archaeological Survey maintains every cave today.

150
00:11:05,233 --> 00:11:10,462
A thousand years of pilgrims, surveyors and conservators found no machines.

151
00:11:10,462 --> 00:11:15,899
What they found were the traces of carvers: tool marks, and work left halfway.

152
00:11:16,366 --> 00:11:18,166
The halls are breathtaking.

153
00:11:18,166 --> 00:11:22,633
But they are rooms carved by people, for worship, not engine rooms.

154
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:24,863
Stage seven: the centuries.

155
00:11:24,863 --> 00:11:31,066
Here our time-lapse surprises us, because the camera doesn't stop when the main shrine is done.

156
00:11:31,533 --> 00:11:33,841
Traces of white plaster survive.

157
00:11:33,841 --> 00:11:45,166
The temple was probably coated white, to look like snowy Mount Kailasa itself, the Himalayan home of Shiva, and its ceilings hold several layers of painting.

158
00:11:45,633 --> 00:11:48,232
Scholars also see different hands.

159
00:11:48,232 --> 00:11:55,646
Dhavalikar dated the famous relief of Ravana shaking Mount Kailasa decades after the main shrine.

160
00:11:55,646 --> 00:12:00,233
Hermann Goetz argued later kings kept extending the complex.

161
00:12:00,700 --> 00:12:10,233
Side shrines were added later, and an eighteenth-century queen, Ahilyabai Holkar, is credited with one of the last layers of paint.

162
00:12:10,233 --> 00:12:14,600
A single eighteen-year sprint is probably the wrong picture.

163
00:12:15,066 --> 00:12:18,195
Even writers from other faiths stood in awe.

164
00:12:18,195 --> 00:12:22,746
A Bijapur historian called it a wonder comparable to Persepolis.

165
00:12:22,746 --> 00:12:27,866
Accounts attributed to the emperor Aurangzeb call it one of the wonders.

166
00:12:28,333 --> 00:12:32,307
And through all of it, this was a temple, carved as an offering.

167
00:12:32,307 --> 00:12:37,833
When we hand the credit to aliens, we take it away from the people who actually did this.

168
00:12:38,299 --> 00:12:41,566
Which brings our time-lapse to its last frame: today.

169
00:12:42,033 --> 00:12:43,250
Stage eight: today.

170
00:12:43,250 --> 00:12:44,339
The camera stops.

171
00:12:44,339 --> 00:12:46,133
Let's score what we've seen.

172
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:50,158
Chisel and pick marks on every surface: evidence.

173
00:12:50,158 --> 00:12:54,007
A royal copper plate crediting Krishnaraja: evidence.

174
00:12:54,007 --> 00:13:01,269
Eight hundred years of rock-cut tradition, and an unfinished top-down twin at Kalugumalai: evidence.

175
00:13:01,269 --> 00:13:05,700
Labour maths that works with a few hundred people: plausible.

176
00:13:06,166 --> 00:13:09,164
Lasers and energy machines: no evidence.

177
00:13:09,164 --> 00:13:14,561
Four hundred thousand tons in eighteen years: exaggerated and unsourced.

178
00:13:14,561 --> 00:13:20,033
The exact place the rubble went, and how long the build took: still open.

179
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:21,365
The verdict.

180
00:13:21,365 --> 00:13:30,601
Kailasa was carved by human hands, under Rashtrakuta kings, by a workshop that inherited centuries of skill and a proven design.

181
00:13:30,601 --> 00:13:38,033
The alien numbers were inflated, and the missing rubble is a question for archaeology, not outer space.

182
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:48,033
What stays open is smaller and more human: exactly how many years it took, how many people worked here, and where they put the hill they took apart.

183
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:54,679
Twelve centuries ago, the copper plate said even the architect wondered how he had done it.

184
00:13:54,679 --> 00:13:56,038
That wonder is fair.

185
00:13:56,038 --> 00:13:57,600
It just belongs to him.

186
00:13:58,066 --> 00:14:00,097
Which stage surprised you most?

187
00:14:00,097 --> 00:14:05,600
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
